21
00:01:51.128 --> 00:01:55.373
Pricing and reserving methods must be owned by qualified actuaries.

22
00:01:56.723 --> 00:01:57.577
Part 1.

23
00:01:57.857 --> 00:01:59.393
The System in One Page.

24
00:02:00.643 --> 00:02:01.965
How an insurer works.

25
00:02:02.245 --> 00:02:05.680
An insurer collects premiums now and pays claims later.

26
00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:07.112
Its software must:

27
00:02:07.392 --> 00:02:12.725
Price each risk correctly: too cheap loses money, too expensive loses customers.

28
00:02:13.005 --> 00:02:19.619
Issue and administer policies exactly as the contract says, for years (life policies for decades).

29
00:02:19.899 --> 00:02:22.907
Pay valid claims fast, and detect invalid ones.

30
00:02:23.187 --> 00:02:28.605
Hold enough reserves for claims not yet reported, and share large risks with reinsurers.

31
00:02:29.955 --> 00:02:33.646
Prove all of this to the regulator, auditors and policyholders.

32
00:02:34.996 --> 00:02:36.084
Main systems.

33
00:02:36.364 --> 00:02:37.729
Here is a quick table:

34
00:02:38.009 --> 00:02:39.524
System, and What it does.

35
00:02:39.804 --> 00:02:41.191
Product configurator:

36
00:02:41.471 --> 00:02:47.316
Defines products: coverages, options, eligibility, rating factors, rules, documents.

37
00:02:47.596 --> 00:02:48.684
Rating engine:

38
00:02:48.964 --> 00:02:52.057
Computes premium from risk factors and product rules.

39
00:02:52.337 --> 00:02:53.383
Distribution:

40
00:02:53.663 --> 00:02:58.356
App or web, agents, brokers, banks (bancassurance), aggregators; commissions.

41
00:02:58.636 --> 00:02:59.959
Quote and underwriting:

42
00:03:00.239 --> 00:03:06.212
Collects risk data, applies rules and scores, decides accept or load or decline or refer.

43
00:03:06.492 --> 00:03:08.711
Policy administration (PAS):

44
00:03:08.991 --> 00:03:14.665
Issuance, policy documents, endorsements, renewals, cancellations, lapses and revivals.

45
00:03:16.015 --> 00:03:17.381
Billing and collections:

46
00:03:17.661 --> 00:03:22.973
Premium payments, instalments, mandates (NACH or UPI AutoPay), grace periods.

47
00:03:23.253 --> 00:03:24.447
Claims management:

48
00:03:24.727 --> 00:03:29.634
Intake, validation, investigation, assessment, approval, payment, recovery.

49
00:03:29.914 --> 00:03:31.642
Health TPA or cashless:

50
00:03:31.922 --> 00:03:36.231
Hospital pre-authorisation, network hospitals, tariffs, bill auditing.

51
00:03:36.511 --> 00:03:38.282
Motor surveys and garages:

52
00:03:38.562 --> 00:03:43.703
Surveyor assignment, photo or video inspection, garage network, repair estimates.

53
00:03:43.983 --> 00:03:45.285
Fraud and SIU:

54
00:03:45.565 --> 00:03:50.834
Fraud scoring, network analysis, the special investigations unit's case management.

55
00:03:51.114 --> 00:03:52.202
Reinsurance:

56
00:03:52.482 --> 00:03:55.042
Treaties, cessions, recoveries, bordereaux.

57
00:03:56.392 --> 00:03:57.928
Actuarial and finance:

58
00:03:58.208 --> 00:04:03.115
Reserves (IBNR), experience analysis, financial and regulatory reporting.

59
00:04:03.395 --> 00:04:04.568
Customer service:

60
00:04:04.848 --> 00:04:07.472
Contact centre, grievances, self-service.

61
00:04:07.752 --> 00:04:09.629
Documents and communications:

62
00:04:09.909 --> 00:04:13.003
Policy wordings, schedules, letters, e-policies.

63
00:04:13.283 --> 00:04:14.456
Data platform:

64
00:04:14.736 --> 00:04:17.275
Warehouse, actuarial data marts, ML.

65
00:04:18.625 --> 00:04:20.779
Scale for this design (assumptions).

66
00:04:21.059 --> 00:04:22.425
Here is a quick table:

67
00:04:22.705 --> 00:04:24.027
Quantity, and Value.

68
00:04:24.307 --> 00:04:25.587
Policies in force:

69
00:04:25.867 --> 00:04:30.774
8M (health 3M, motor 4.5M, life 0.5M).

70
00:04:31.054 --> 00:04:32.398
New policies per day:

71
00:04:32.678 --> 00:04:36.369
20k; quotes per day 1M (aggregators generate many).

72
00:04:36.649 --> 00:04:39.038
Renewals per day: ~15k.

73
00:04:39.318 --> 00:04:46.678
Claims per day: ~8k (health cashless dominate), with seasonal and catastrophe spikes (floods, epidemics).

74
00:04:46.958 --> 00:04:52.483
Documents: ~50M documents per year (proposals, bills, photos, reports).

75
00:04:53.833 --> 00:04:55.220
Estimation highlights:

76
00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:59.788
Quotes dwarf policies (50:1), especially from aggregators.

77
00:05:00.068 --> 00:05:02.628
The rating engine must be fast and cacheable.

78
00:05:02.908 --> 00:05:05.980
Quote storage has short retention unless converted.

79
00:05:06.260 --> 00:05:08.159
Claims are the operational heart.

80
00:05:08.439 --> 00:05:12.791
Each claim involves documents, people and payments over days or weeks.

81
00:05:13.071 --> 00:05:16.015
That makes it a workflow problem more than a throughput one.

82
00:05:17.365 --> 00:05:24.426
Catastrophes create sudden claim volumes: motor and home claims after a flood can reach ten times normal in a week.

83
00:05:24.706 --> 00:05:28.034
They need surge operations and simplified processes.

84
00:05:29.384 --> 00:05:30.259
Part 2.

85
00:05:30.539 --> 00:05:31.499
Requirements.

86
00:05:32.749 --> 00:05:33.602
Functional.

87
00:05:33.882 --> 00:05:41.391
Launch new products and variants quickly by configuration, after regulatory approval (VERIFY the file-and-use process).

88
00:05:41.671 --> 00:05:44.509
Quote in under a second for aggregators and the app.

89
00:05:44.789 --> 00:05:47.157
Buy online with e-KYC and payment.

90
00:05:47.437 --> 00:05:49.698
Receive the policy document instantly.

91
00:05:49.978 --> 00:05:59.407
Underwrite automatically where possible (straight-through), refer complex cases to human underwriters, request medical tests for life and health when needed.

92
00:05:59.687 --> 00:06:06.855
Handle mid-term changes (add a family member, change a vehicle, change a nominee) with pro-rata premium adjustments.

93
00:06:08.205 --> 00:06:12.941
Renewals with reminders, auto-debit, and no-claim bonus or discount rules.

94
00:06:13.221 --> 00:06:17.189
Life policies: premium due, grace periods, lapse, revival.

95
00:06:17.469 --> 00:06:30.227
Claims: health cashless (hospital-initiated pre-auth) and reimbursement (customer uploads bills);. motor own-damage and third party, with surveys;. life death claims with nominee verification.

96
00:06:30.507 --> 00:06:32.661
All with status tracking and timelines.

97
00:06:34.011 --> 00:06:36.827
Fraud detection and investigation workflows.

98
00:06:37.107 --> 00:06:41.737
Reinsurance cessions and recoveries; reserves; regulatory returns.

99
00:06:42.017 --> 00:06:45.921
Grievances and ombudsman escalations (VERIFY processes).

100
00:06:47.271 --> 00:06:48.380
Non-functional.

101
00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:50.025
Here is a quick table:

102
00:06:50.305 --> 00:06:51.671
Attribute, and Target.

103
00:06:51.951 --> 00:06:59.503
Quote latency: p95 500 ms including external lookups (vehicle registry, KYC checks).

104
00:06:59.783 --> 00:07:00.700
Correctness:

105
00:07:00.980 --> 00:07:06.783
Premiums, endorsements and claim payments exactly per contract terms; every change versioned.

106
00:07:07.063 --> 00:07:08.044
Auditability:

107
00:07:08.324 --> 00:07:12.761
Full history of every policy and claim decision with who or what or why.

108
00:07:13.041 --> 00:07:14.023
Longevity:

109
00:07:14.303 --> 00:07:18.441
Policies administered for decades; product versions preserved forever.

110
00:07:18.721 --> 00:07:19.788
Availability:

111
00:07:20.068 --> 00:07:29.604
Sales channels and cashless approvals ≥ 99.9%; hospital pre-auth within regulatory timelines (VERIFY).

112
00:07:29.884 --> 00:07:30.801
Privacy:

113
00:07:31.081 --> 00:07:36.863
Health and personal data protected; consent-based data use (DPDP Act; VERIFY).

114
00:07:38.213 --> 00:07:39.109
Part 3.

115
00:07:39.389 --> 00:07:40.925
High-Level Architecture.

116
00:07:42.175 --> 00:07:43.519
Here is the diagram.

117
00:07:47.369 --> 00:07:48.435
Key decisions:

118
00:07:49.785 --> 00:07:51.151
Here is a quick table:

119
00:07:51.431 --> 00:07:53.201
Decision, and Choice, and Why.

120
00:07:53.481 --> 00:07:55.188
Products as configuration.

121
00:07:55.468 --> 00:07:56.300
Choice:

122
00:07:56.580 --> 00:08:06.351
A product configurator with versioned product definitions (coverages, rules, rate tables) interpreted by the rating, underwriting and policy engines.

123
00:08:06.631 --> 00:08:07.313
Why:

124
00:08:07.593 --> 00:08:12.500
New products without code changes; old versions preserved for in-force policies.

125
00:08:12.780 --> 00:08:14.124
Policy data model.

126
00:08:14.404 --> 00:08:15.236
Choice:

127
00:08:15.516 --> 00:08:21.489
Bitemporal: effective dates (when a change applies in the contract) and recorded dates (when we learned it).

128
00:08:21.769 --> 00:08:22.452
Why:

129
00:08:22.732 --> 00:08:25.697
Back-dated endorsements, audits, what did we know when.

130
00:08:25.977 --> 00:08:26.788
Claims.

131
00:08:27.068 --> 00:08:27.900
Choice:

132
00:08:28.180 --> 00:08:31.657
Workflow engine with human tasks, SLAs and rules.

133
00:08:31.937 --> 00:08:32.620
Why:

134
00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:36.335
Long-running, multi-party processes (Ch 50).

135
00:08:37.685 --> 00:08:38.922
Buy versus build.

136
00:08:39.202 --> 00:08:40.034
Choice:

137
00:08:40.314 --> 00:08:48.869
Many insurers buy a core suite (policy or billing or claims) and build digital channels, rating services, fraud and data around it.

138
00:08:49.149 --> 00:08:49.831
Why:

139
00:08:50.111 --> 00:08:54.677
Mature domain, regulatory features; differentiation is at the edges.

140
00:08:54.957 --> 00:08:55.959
Integration.

141
00:08:56.239 --> 00:08:57.071
Choice:

142
00:08:57.351 --> 00:09:03.645
APIs for partners, events internally, file exchanges where industry partners require them.

143
00:09:03.925 --> 00:09:04.607
Why:

144
00:09:04.887 --> 00:09:06.125
Real-world mix.

145
00:09:07.475 --> 00:09:08.392
Part 4.

146
00:09:08.672 --> 00:09:10.549
How It Works, Journeys End to End.

147
00:09:11.799 --> 00:09:14.871
Journey 1: buying motor insurance on an aggregator.

148
00:09:15.151 --> 00:09:23.322
Quote request: the customer enters their vehicle registration number on an aggregator site, which calls the insurer's quote API.

149
00:09:23.602 --> 00:09:40.818
Enrichment: the insurer fetches vehicle details (make, model, variant, fuel, registration date) from a vehicle registry integration (VERIFY data-access arrangements);. checks previous insurance and claims history through industry data where available (VERIFY);.

150
00:09:42.168 --> 00:09:46.797
looks up the insured declared value (IDV) from depreciation schedules.

151
00:09:47.077 --> 00:09:54.395
Rating (§6.1): base premium from rate tables by vehicle class, zone and age, then adjustments:

152
00:09:54.675 --> 00:10:06.643
No Claim Bonus carried from the previous policy (VERIFY current NCB slabs);. add-ons (zero depreciation, engine protection, roadside assistance);. voluntary deductible;.

153
00:10:07.993 --> 00:10:13.859
the insurer's pricing model loading or discount within filed ranges;. taxes (GST).

154
00:10:14.139 --> 00:10:17.510
Third-party premium is set by the regulator (VERIFY).

155
00:10:17.790 --> 00:10:23.934
Response: the quote returns within hundreds of milliseconds with a quote ID valid for a short time.

156
00:10:24.214 --> 00:10:27.158
Aggregators may show 20 insurers side by side.

157
00:10:27.438 --> 00:10:29.849
Purchase: the customer selects and pays.

158
00:10:30.129 --> 00:10:36.507
KYC is mandatory for all policies now (VERIFY), done with CKYC lookup or e-KYC.

159
00:10:37.857 --> 00:10:46.092
Break-in cases (expired policy) need a self-inspection: the customer records a guided video or photos of the car in the app.

160
00:10:46.372 --> 00:10:52.644
An image model checks for damage and fraud (§6.5), and a human reviews exceptions.

161
00:10:52.924 --> 00:10:59.729
Issuance: underwriting rules pass;. the policy is created in PAS;. the premium is recorded in billing;.

162
00:11:01.079 --> 00:11:08.973
commission is accrued for the aggregator;. the policy document is generated (schedule plus wordings) and emailed instantly.

163
00:11:09.253 --> 00:11:19.791
Downstream: the policy is reported to industry and regulatory repositories as required (VERIFY), and becomes a reinsurance cession if a treaty covers it.

164
00:11:21.141 --> 00:11:24.107
Journey 2: buying a family health policy in the app.

165
00:11:24.387 --> 00:11:30.381
Proposal: the customer adds members (ages, pre-existing conditions, lifestyle questions).

166
00:11:30.661 --> 00:11:47.216
Underwriting rules: accept at standard rates (young, no conditions);. accept with loading or a waiting period for declared conditions;. refer to medical underwriting (tele-medical interview or medical tests at partner labs);. decline outside appetite.

167
00:11:48.566 --> 00:11:52.299
A score (§6.3) orders referrals by risk.

168
00:11:52.579 --> 00:11:58.830
Pricing: premium by age band per member, sum insured, zone (city tier), deductibles, add-ons.

169
00:11:59.110 --> 00:12:01.350
The family floater discount is applied.

170
00:12:01.630 --> 00:12:07.817
Payment and mandate: annual payment, or instalments via NACH or UPI AutoPay mandates.

171
00:12:08.097 --> 00:12:12.619
Issuance: the policy with waiting periods recorded per member and condition.

172
00:12:12.899 --> 00:12:16.014
A health card is issued (digital) for cashless use.

173
00:12:17.364 --> 00:12:26.409
Free-look period: the customer can cancel within a set number of days for a refund minus proportionate costs (VERIFY the current period and rules).

174
00:12:26.689 --> 00:12:29.633
The PAS supports this as a special cancellation.

175
00:12:30.983 --> 00:12:33.394
Journey 3: a cashless hospital claim.

176
00:12:33.674 --> 00:12:38.389
Admission and pre-auth request: the patient is admitted to a network hospital.

177
00:12:38.669 --> 00:12:47.287
The hospital's insurance desk sends a pre-authorisation request with the diagnosis, planned treatment, estimated cost and doctor notes.

178
00:12:47.567 --> 00:13:00.239
It arrives via the insurer's TPA portal or API or the National Health Claims Exchange (NHCX, FHIR-based; VERIFY adoption) (I04 §4.4).

179
00:13:00.519 --> 00:13:07.538
Coverage checks (automated first): policy in force;. member covered;. waiting periods passed for this condition;.

180
00:13:08.888 --> 00:13:19.683
sum insured available (after previous claims);. sub-limits (room rent caps, disease-specific limits);. exclusions;. hospital in network with an agreed package tariff.

181
00:13:19.963 --> 00:13:27.365
Medical adjudication: rules auto-approve standard packages (for example, cataract surgery at the agreed tariff);.

182
00:13:28.715 --> 00:13:37.590
otherwise a medical officer reviews: are the treatment and length of stay appropriate for the diagnosis? queries go back to the hospital as tasks.

183
00:13:37.870 --> 00:13:42.393
Approval: within the regulatory timeline for pre-auth decisions (VERIFY).

184
00:13:42.673 --> 00:13:46.747
An approved amount is issued, with enhancements if the stay extends.

185
00:13:47.027 --> 00:13:52.531
Discharge: the hospital submits the final bill with itemisation and discharge summary;.

186
00:13:53.881 --> 00:14:11.673
bill audit (§6.7) checks items against the tariff, flags non-payables (consumables excluded by policy) and applies co-pay and deductibles;. final approval is issued, again within timelines (VERIFY);. the patient pays the non-payables and leaves.

187
00:14:11.953 --> 00:14:17.841
Settlement: the insurer pays the hospital via bank transfer in batches, reconciled per claim.

188
00:14:18.121 --> 00:14:28.809
Accounting: the claim updates the sum insured utilisation, the no-claim bonus status for renewal, reserves (§6.8) and reinsurance recoveries.

189
00:14:30.159 --> 00:14:32.442
Journey 4: a motor accident claim.

190
00:14:32.722 --> 00:14:41.085
FNOL (first notice of loss): in the app, the customer records the accident location, photos and description, and chooses a garage.

191
00:14:41.365 --> 00:14:56.319
Triage: small, simple claims with clear photos go to AI-assisted assessment: damage detection and estimate by part (§6.5), within a threshold;. larger or suspicious claims get a surveyor (licensed,

192
00:14:56.599 --> 00:15:09.485
per regulation; VERIFY thresholds for mandatory surveys);. third-party injury claims follow legal processes (motor accident claims tribunals) and are handled separately, with long durations.

193
00:15:10.835 --> 00:15:30.547
Survey: the surveyor inspects, virtually or in person, and records findings in the surveyor app;. the garage submits a repair estimate;. the system compares the estimate to part price catalogues and labour norms;. depreciation is applied per part material, unless a zero-depreciation add-on applies.

194
00:15:31.897 --> 00:15:34.009
Approval: the repair is authorised.

195
00:15:34.289 --> 00:15:37.126
After repair, a re-inspection (photos) is done.

196
00:15:37.406 --> 00:15:42.825
The insurer pays the garage directly (cashless garage), or reimburses the customer.

197
00:15:43.105 --> 00:15:54.838
Fraud checks throughout (§6.6): inconsistent photos, previously claimed damage (image similarity against past claims), staged accident patterns, linked parties.

198
00:15:55.118 --> 00:16:03.694
Recovery: salvage for total losses (the vehicle is sold via auction);. subrogation against a third party's insurer where applicable.

199
00:16:05.044 --> 00:16:07.540
Journey 5: a life insurance death claim.

200
00:16:07.820 --> 00:16:18.188
Intimation: the nominee intimates the claim via the app, branch or call centre, with the death certificate, policy details, and their own KYC and bank details.

201
00:16:18.468 --> 00:16:24.377
Validation: policy in force at the date of death, no lapse, and nominee identity verified.

202
00:16:24.657 --> 00:16:37.052
Early claims (for example, within the first few years) may be investigated for non-disclosure, within legal limits on contestability (VERIFY Section 45 of the Insurance Act as amended).

203
00:16:38.402 --> 00:16:47.575
Decision and payment: payment to the nominee's verified account within regulatory timelines, with interest for delays where required (VERIFY).

204
00:16:47.855 --> 00:16:50.970
Reinsurance recovery for amounts above retention.

205
00:16:52.320 --> 00:16:54.261
Journey 6: renewal and lapse.

206
00:16:54.541 --> 00:16:59.917
Reminders: renewal notices go out before expiry by email, SMS and app.

207
00:17:00.197 --> 00:17:08.197
For motor and health, the renewal premium is recalculated (age band change, NCB, claims experience, rate revisions).

208
00:17:08.477 --> 00:17:15.411
Auto-renewal: where a mandate exists, the premium is debited after the pre-debit notification (VERIFY rules).

209
00:17:15.691 --> 00:17:21.557
Grace periods: during the grace period, cover continuity rules apply per product (VERIFY).

210
00:17:21.837 --> 00:17:24.803
Health: continuity preserves waiting-period credit.

211
00:17:26.153 --> 00:17:29.779
Life: unpaid premiums lead to lapse after the grace period.

212
00:17:30.059 --> 00:17:35.819
Revival is possible within a window, with arrears plus interest and possibly fresh underwriting.

213
00:17:36.099 --> 00:17:41.369
Lapse prediction (§6.9) triggers targeted outreach before due dates.

214
00:17:42.719 --> 00:17:43.679
Part 5.

215
00:17:43.959 --> 00:17:45.495
Core Subsystems in Depth.

216
00:17:46.745 --> 00:17:48.153
Product configurator.

217
00:17:48.433 --> 00:18:00.870
A product definition includes: coverages and benefits;. options and add-ons;. eligibility rules;. rating factors and rate tables;. underwriting rules;. policy wording templates;.

218
00:18:02.220 --> 00:18:09.452
claims rules (waiting periods, sub-limits, exclusions, co-pays);. commission rules;. regulatory identifiers.

219
00:18:09.732 --> 00:18:11.396
Versioned with effective dates.

220
00:18:11.676 --> 00:18:17.628
A policy is always administered under the product version it was sold on, or renewed into, per rules.

221
00:18:17.908 --> 00:18:24.991
Output: a machine-readable product model interpreted by the rating engine, the underwriting engine, PAS and claims.

222
00:18:25.271 --> 00:18:30.881
One definition, many consumers, which avoids inconsistencies between quoting and claims.

223
00:18:32.231 --> 00:18:41.639
Testing: every product version has test cases (premium examples filed with the regulator, claims examples) run automatically before release.

224
00:18:42.989 --> 00:18:44.077
Rating engine.

225
00:18:44.357 --> 00:18:45.616
Stateless and fast.

226
00:18:45.896 --> 00:18:48.328
Input: risk data + product version.

227
00:18:48.608 --> 00:18:52.853
Output: premium breakdown (per coverage, loadings, discounts, taxes).

228
00:18:53.133 --> 00:18:55.203
Rate tables are loaded in memory.

229
00:18:55.483 --> 00:19:00.816
Pricing models (§6.1) are served as tables or model artefacts.

230
00:19:01.096 --> 00:19:04.957
Aggregator traffic: very high quote volumes with low conversion.

231
00:19:05.237 --> 00:19:12.043
Cache quotes by risk signature (vehicle variant + zone + age + NCB + add-ons) for minutes.

232
00:19:12.323 --> 00:19:13.795
Rate-limit per partner.

233
00:19:14.075 --> 00:19:20.325
Explainability: every quote stores its factor contributions, for disputes and regulatory review.

234
00:19:21.675 --> 00:19:24.470
Policy administration with bitemporal data.

235
00:19:24.750 --> 00:19:35.886
Policy versions: each change (endorsement) creates a new version with an effective date range (when the change applies in the contract) and a transaction time (when it was recorded).

236
00:19:36.166 --> 00:19:45.403
Back-dated changes (for example, a vehicle change effective last week) produce premium adjustments computed pro-rata for the affected period.

237
00:19:45.683 --> 00:19:54.131
Queries: what cover applied on the accident date? uses effective time;. what did we know when we approved that claim? uses transaction time.

238
00:19:55.481 --> 00:19:58.895
Documents: endorsement schedules generated per version.

239
00:19:59.175 --> 00:20:02.972
Cancellations: pro-rata or short-period scales per rules.

240
00:20:03.252 --> 00:20:05.193
Refund computation and payment.

241
00:20:06.543 --> 00:20:07.887
Billing and collections.

242
00:20:08.167 --> 00:20:18.727
Payment modes: single premium, instalments (monthly or quarterly), mandates (NACH, UPI AutoPay; VERIFY limits and pre-debit rules), card, net banking.

243
00:20:19.007 --> 00:20:25.706
Premium allocation: premium receivable accounts per policy; unallocated receipts handled by rules.

244
00:20:25.986 --> 00:20:32.834
Dunning: failed debits, then retry, then notify, then grace period, then lapse or cancellation per product.

245
00:20:33.114 --> 00:20:38.639
Integration with finance: premium income recognition, GST, commission payable.

246
00:20:39.989 --> 00:20:41.227
Claims management.

247
00:20:41.507 --> 00:20:55.928
A claim is a case with a workflow: intake (FNOL);. coverage verification;. triage (complexity, fraud score, value);. assignment (automation, adjuster, surveyor, medical officer);. investigation;. assessment;.

248
00:20:57.278 --> 00:21:06.473
approval (authority limits by role and amount);. payment;. recovery (reinsurance, salvage, subrogation);. closure, and reopen if needed.

249
00:21:06.753 --> 00:21:08.438
Human tasks with SLAs.

250
00:21:08.718 --> 00:21:11.449
Authority matrices (who can approve how much).

251
00:21:11.729 --> 00:21:14.246
Maker-checker for payments above thresholds.

252
00:21:15.596 --> 00:21:22.465
Reserves per claim: an initial reserve at intimation (by triage model), updated as information arrives.

253
00:21:22.745 --> 00:21:25.455
These case reserves feed actuarial reserving.

254
00:21:25.735 --> 00:21:38.748
Straight-through processing (STP): simple claims (low value, clear coverage, low fraud score) auto-approve and pay within minutes, for example, small OPD health claims or windshield replacement.

255
00:21:39.028 --> 00:21:46.047
Communication: status updates to customers and hospitals, and document requests as tasks visible in the app.

256
00:21:47.397 --> 00:21:48.463
Reinsurance.

257
00:21:48.743 --> 00:22:04.167
Treaties: quota share: a fixed percentage of every policy ceded;. surplus: amounts above the retention ceded, per policy;. excess of loss: the reinsurer pays losses above a threshold per event or per risk;. catastrophe covers.

258
00:22:04.447 --> 00:22:09.567
Cessions: computed per policy (at issuance and endorsement), with cession premiums.

259
00:22:10.917 --> 00:22:16.229
Recoveries: computed per claim or per event (catastrophes aggregate many claims).

260
00:22:16.509 --> 00:22:19.987
Bordereaux (listings) are sent to reinsurers periodically.

261
00:22:20.267 --> 00:22:22.763
Reinsurance accounting is reconciled.

262
00:22:23.043 --> 00:22:29.933
Facultative reinsurance for individual large risks outside treaties (a workflow with reinsurer quotes).

263
00:22:31.283 --> 00:22:32.478
Fraud management.

264
00:22:32.758 --> 00:22:38.945
Scoring at key points: policy issuance (application fraud), claim intimation, and before payment.

265
00:22:39.225 --> 00:22:49.593
Rules: claims soon after inception;. high-value claims just below approval thresholds;. repeated claimants;. hospitals or garages with outlier patterns.

266
00:22:49.873 --> 00:22:54.609
Models: supervised (from past confirmed fraud) plus anomaly detection.

267
00:22:55.959 --> 00:23:05.559
Network analysis (§6.6): links between claimants, garages, hospitals, doctors, phones, bank accounts and addresses reveal rings.

268
00:23:05.839 --> 00:23:12.153
SIU case management: investigation tasks, evidence, outcomes that feed back as labels.

269
00:23:12.433 --> 00:23:20.199
Industry sharing: fraud repositories and claims history databases shared across insurers, where regulated (VERIFY).

270
00:23:21.549 --> 00:23:22.487
Part 6.

271
00:23:22.767 --> 00:23:23.727
Algorithms.

272
00:23:24.977 --> 00:23:27.217
Pricing with generalised linear models.

273
00:23:27.497 --> 00:23:43.241
Frequency times severity: model claim frequency (claims per policy-year) with a Poisson GLM (log link);. model claim severity (cost per claim) with a Gamma GLM (log link);. pure premium = frequency times severity.

274
00:23:43.521 --> 00:23:51.415
With log links, factors multiply: premium = base times factor(age) times factor(zone) times factor(vehicle)...

275
00:23:51.695 --> 00:23:54.553
Tweedie GLMs model pure premium directly.

276
00:23:55.903 --> 00:24:05.951
Credibility and smoothing: sparse cells are smoothed, for example, a rare vehicle model gets credibility-weighted factors between its own experience and its group's.

277
00:24:06.231 --> 00:24:10.989
Gradient-boosted models capture interactions but must be explainable and filed.

278
00:24:11.269 --> 00:24:21.658
Many insurers use GBMs to find interactions, then encode them in GLM-style tables for filing and transparency (VERIFY local product filing rules).

279
00:24:21.938 --> 00:24:30.322
Final premium: pure premium + expense loadings + profit margin + commissions + taxes, within approved rate ranges.

280
00:24:31.672 --> 00:24:32.845
Rules engines.

281
00:24:33.125 --> 00:24:42.405
Underwriting, eligibility and claims coverage rules are expressed declaratively: if age 55 and sum insured 50 lakh then refer.

282
00:24:42.685 --> 00:24:51.923
Engines: the Rete algorithm (for example, Drools) evaluates many rules efficiently by sharing conditions and incrementally matching facts.

283
00:24:52.203 --> 00:24:54.613
Decision tables are for business users.

284
00:24:54.893 --> 00:25:02.339
Governance: versioned rule sets tied to product versions, test suites, and audit of which rules fired for each decision.

285
00:25:03.689 --> 00:25:04.969
Underwriting scoring.

286
00:25:05.249 --> 00:25:18.113
A risk score from applicant data (age, BMI, declared conditions, occupation, income-to-cover ratio), external data (bureau-based signals where permitted; VERIFY), and medical test results.

287
00:25:18.393 --> 00:25:25.369
Decisions: thresholds map the score to bands (accept, loading, referral, decline), with rules as guard rails.

288
00:25:25.649 --> 00:25:29.809
Accelerated underwriting: lower-risk applicants skip medical tests.

289
00:25:30.089 --> 00:25:36.169
The model must control mortality or morbidity slippage, monitored against actual claims experience.

290
00:25:37.519 --> 00:25:43.833
Fairness: exclude prohibited factors; monitor outcomes across groups (VERIFY applicable rules).

291
00:25:45.183 --> 00:25:47.743
Telematics (usage-based motor insurance).

292
00:25:48.023 --> 00:25:57.687
Data: trips from a phone app or device: distance, time of day, speeding relative to road limits, harsh braking and acceleration, phone use while driving.

293
00:25:57.967 --> 00:26:03.002
Driving score: event rates per 100 km weighted by severity and exposure.

294
00:26:03.282 --> 00:26:09.234
Premium discounts are tied to the score (VERIFY regulatory framework for pay-as-you-drive products).

295
00:26:09.514 --> 00:26:21.098
Pipeline: sensor streams, then trip segmentation, then map matching (I05 §6.5), then event detection, then aggregation per policy period.

296
00:26:22.448 --> 00:26:24.197
Image-based damage assessment.

297
00:26:24.477 --> 00:26:27.229
Guided capture ensures angles and coverage.

298
00:26:27.509 --> 00:26:31.755
Photo metadata checks: time, location, device consistency.

299
00:26:32.035 --> 00:26:43.235
Computer vision models: part detection (bumper, door, headlamp);. damage detection and classification (scratch, dent, crack);. severity, then repair versus replace decisions.

300
00:26:43.515 --> 00:26:48.635
Estimate: parts price catalogue + labour norms + paint, then estimated cost.

301
00:26:48.915 --> 00:26:51.816
The confidence decides auto-approve or survey.

302
00:26:53.166 --> 00:27:04.878
Fraud: perceptual hashing against past claim photos (reused images; I07 §6.8);. edited image detection;. odometer and plate consistency.

303
00:27:06.228 --> 00:27:07.337
Fraud networks.

304
00:27:07.617 --> 00:27:18.071
Graph: nodes = policyholders, claims, vehicles, garages, hospitals, doctors, phones, emails, bank accounts, addresses; edges = relationships.

305
00:27:18.351 --> 00:27:31.855
Detection: connected components and community detection find rings;. centrality finds hubs (a garage linked to many suspicious claims);. graph features (degree, shared attributes) feed supervised models.

306
00:27:32.135 --> 00:27:39.687
Entity resolution first: the same person with different spellings or phones (I04 §6.1 patterns).

307
00:27:41.037 --> 00:27:42.850
Claims triage and bill auditing.

308
00:27:43.130 --> 00:27:48.463
Triage model: predicts complexity, expected cost and fraud risk at intimation.

309
00:27:48.743 --> 00:27:55.122
It routes to STP, a junior or senior adjuster, or the SIU, and sets the initial reserve.

310
00:27:55.402 --> 00:28:13.045
Health bill audit: map bill items to standard codes;. compare with the package tariff and reasonable-cost benchmarks for the diagnosis and city;. flag non-payable consumables per regulatory lists (VERIFY), duplicate charges, and room-rent proportionate deductions.

311
00:28:14.395 --> 00:28:19.003
Length-of-stay benchmarks per diagnosis flag outliers for medical review.

312
00:28:20.353 --> 00:28:22.913
Reserving (IBNR) with the chain-ladder method.

313
00:28:23.193 --> 00:28:30.233
The problem: at any date, some claims have happened but aren't reported yet (IBNR), and reported claims will still develop.

314
00:28:30.513 --> 00:28:32.689
Reserves must cover the ultimate cost.

315
00:28:32.969 --> 00:28:51.571
Chain ladder: arrange paid (or incurred) claims in a triangle: rows = accident period, columns = development period;. compute age-to-age development factors as the ratio of cumulative claims between consecutive development periods, weighted across accident periods;.

316
00:28:52.921 --> 00:28:59.343
multiply the latest diagonal by the remaining factors to project ultimate claims per accident period;.

317
00:28:59.623 --> 00:29:02.119
IBNR = ultimate minus reported.

318
00:29:02.399 --> 00:29:03.273
Variants:

319
00:29:03.553 --> 00:29:12.215
Bornhuetter, Ferguson (blending expected loss ratio for immature periods), Mack's method for uncertainty, stochastic bootstraps.

320
00:29:12.495 --> 00:29:20.132
Systems role: the data platform produces clean triangles by product, line and period, reconciled with finance.

321
00:29:20.412 --> 00:29:22.503
Actuaries run and sign off models.

322
00:29:23.853 --> 00:29:25.517
Lapse and renewal prediction.

323
00:29:25.797 --> 00:29:33.413
Model: classifier on payment history, tenure, channel, premium change at renewal, engagement and claims experience.

324
00:29:33.693 --> 00:29:37.703
Use: targeted reminders, agent follow-ups, offering instalments.

325
00:29:37.983 --> 00:29:39.839
Measured by uplift experiments.

