456
00:35:26.140 --> 00:35:34.865
grievance redressal with escalation to the Bima Bharosa system and ombudsman;. disclosure of rejection reasons (VERIFY current rules).

457
00:35:35.145 --> 00:35:42.356
Distribution compliance: commission and expense limits, disclosure by intermediaries, aggregator rules (VERIFY).

458
00:35:42.636 --> 00:35:43.937
Industry platforms:

459
00:35:44.217 --> 00:35:53.583
Bima Sugam, an IRDAI-backed insurance marketplace platform in development, and insurance repositories for e-policies (VERIFY current status).

460
00:35:53.863 --> 00:35:55.057
Cybersecurity:

461
00:35:55.337 --> 00:35:58.943
IRDAI information and cyber security guidelines (VERIFY).

462
00:36:00.293 --> 00:36:01.146
Part 10.

463
00:36:01.426 --> 00:36:03.346
Reliability and Operations.

464
00:36:04.596 --> 00:36:09.929
Channel availability: quote and buy APIs are horizontally scaled and stateless.

465
00:36:10.209 --> 00:36:13.025
Aggregator traffic is rate-limited per partner.

466
00:36:13.305 --> 00:36:14.521
Rating is cached.

467
00:36:14.801 --> 00:36:17.937
Cashless approvals must work 24 times 7.

468
00:36:18.217 --> 00:36:22.228
The TPA and medical officer workflow needs on-call coverage.

469
00:36:22.508 --> 00:36:25.153
System outages delay patient discharges.

470
00:36:25.433 --> 00:36:37.423
Catastrophe mode: after floods or cyclones, activate simplified claims processes: self-survey with photos;. relaxed documentation per regulator advisories (VERIFY);.

471
00:36:38.773 --> 00:36:45.855
surge surveyor allocation;. dedicated dashboards;. reserve estimates per event for reinsurance notification.

472
00:36:46.135 --> 00:36:55.927
Month-end and year-end: closing processes (earned premium, reserves, reinsurance accounting) run as scheduled pipelines with reconciliation checks.

473
00:36:56.207 --> 00:37:01.882
Data quality: exposure and claims data errors directly distort pricing and reserves.

474
00:37:02.162 --> 00:37:06.301
Build validation rules into pipelines and reconcile with finance.

475
00:37:07.651 --> 00:37:08.653
Part 11.

476
00:37:08.933 --> 00:37:09.957
Key Trade-offs.

477
00:37:11.207 --> 00:37:12.573
Here is a quick table:

478
00:37:12.853 --> 00:37:15.370
Decision, and Chosen, and Accepted cost.

479
00:37:15.650 --> 00:37:16.695
Core systems.

480
00:37:16.975 --> 00:37:17.807
Chosen:

481
00:37:18.087 --> 00:37:19.943
Buy a suite, build the edges.

482
00:37:20.223 --> 00:37:21.397
Accepted cost:

483
00:37:21.677 --> 00:37:23.895
Vendor constraints; integration work.

484
00:37:24.175 --> 00:37:25.455
Product definition.

485
00:37:25.735 --> 00:37:26.567
Chosen:

486
00:37:26.847 --> 00:37:28.789
Configuration-driven, versioned.

487
00:37:29.069 --> 00:37:30.242
Accepted cost:

488
00:37:30.522 --> 00:37:32.101
Configurator complexity.

489
00:37:32.381 --> 00:37:33.469
Policy data.

490
00:37:33.749 --> 00:37:34.581
Chosen:

491
00:37:34.861 --> 00:37:35.799
Bitemporal.

492
00:37:36.079 --> 00:37:37.253
Accepted cost:

493
00:37:37.533 --> 00:37:39.133
Harder queries and storage.

494
00:37:39.413 --> 00:37:40.223
Claims.

495
00:37:40.503 --> 00:37:41.335
Chosen:

496
00:37:41.615 --> 00:37:44.517
Workflows with STP for simple cases.

497
00:37:44.797 --> 00:37:45.970
Accepted cost:

498
00:37:46.250 --> 00:37:48.106
Model risk in auto-approvals.

499
00:37:48.386 --> 00:37:49.218
Pricing.

500
00:37:49.498 --> 00:37:50.330
Chosen:

501
00:37:50.610 --> 00:37:53.127
GLM tables (with ML insights).

502
00:37:53.407 --> 00:37:54.581
Accepted cost:

503
00:37:54.861 --> 00:37:56.589
Less flexible than pure ML.

504
00:37:56.869 --> 00:37:57.679
Fraud.

505
00:37:57.959 --> 00:37:58.791
Chosen:

506
00:37:59.071 --> 00:38:01.781
Rules + models + graphs + SIU.

507
00:38:02.061 --> 00:38:03.234
Accepted cost:

508
00:38:03.514 --> 00:38:06.074
Investigation cost; false positives.

509
00:38:06.354 --> 00:38:07.186
Reserving.

510
00:38:07.466 --> 00:38:08.298
Chosen:

511
00:38:08.578 --> 00:38:10.946
Actuarial models on clean triangles.

512
00:38:11.226 --> 00:38:12.399
Accepted cost:

513
00:38:12.679 --> 00:38:14.215
Data engineering effort.

514
00:38:15.565 --> 00:38:16.504
Part 12.

515
00:38:16.784 --> 00:38:18.128
Interview Q and A.

516
00:38:19.378 --> 00:38:20.210
Beginner.

517
00:38:20.490 --> 00:38:21.514
Question 1.

518
00:38:21.794 --> 00:38:24.311
What are the core systems of an insurance company?

519
00:38:24.591 --> 00:38:25.957
Thirty-second answer:

520
00:38:26.237 --> 00:38:44.690
Product configuration and rating, distribution channels, underwriting, policy administration (issuance, endorsements, renewals), billing and collections, claims management, fraud, reinsurance, actuarial and finance, customer service, and a data platform for reporting and pricing.

521
00:38:46.040 --> 00:38:47.043
Question 2.

522
00:38:47.323 --> 00:38:50.011
How is a motor insurance premium calculated?

523
00:38:50.291 --> 00:38:51.656
Thirty-second answer:

524
00:38:51.936 --> 00:39:06.016
From vehicle details (class, age, value), zone, the insured declared value, no-claim bonus, add-ons and deductibles through rate tables and model-based loadings, plus the regulated third-party premium and taxes.

525
00:39:07.366 --> 00:39:08.411
Question 3.

526
00:39:08.691 --> 00:39:10.974
What happens in a cashless hospital claim?

527
00:39:11.254 --> 00:39:12.619
Thirty-second answer:

528
00:39:12.899 --> 00:39:31.929
The hospital requests pre-authorisation; the insurer checks policy coverage, waiting periods, limits and network tariffs; a medical officer reviews if needed; an approved amount is issued; at discharge the final bill is audited; the insurer pays the hospital and the patient pays non-payables.

529
00:39:33.279 --> 00:39:34.345
Question 4.

530
00:39:34.625 --> 00:39:35.905
What is an endorsement?

531
00:39:36.185 --> 00:39:37.551
Thirty-second answer:

532
00:39:37.831 --> 00:39:49.052
A mid-term change to a policy, adding a member, changing a vehicle or address, creating a new policy version with an effective date and, often, a pro-rata premium adjustment.

533
00:39:50.402 --> 00:39:51.405
Intermediate.

534
00:39:51.685 --> 00:39:52.794
Question 5.

535
00:39:53.074 --> 00:39:55.826
Why use a bitemporal data model for policies?

536
00:39:56.106 --> 00:39:57.471
Thirty-second answer:

537
00:39:57.751 --> 00:40:13.815
Changes can apply from a past or future date (effective time) and are recorded at a different time (transaction time); claims need what cover applied on the loss date and audits need what did we know when we decided, both require keeping both timelines.

538
00:40:15.165 --> 00:40:16.275
Question 6.

539
00:40:16.555 --> 00:40:18.944
How do you launch new insurance products quickly?

540
00:40:19.224 --> 00:40:20.589
Thirty-second answer:

541
00:40:20.869 --> 00:40:38.043
A product configurator with versioned definitions (coverages, rules, rate tables, wordings, claims rules) consumed by rating, underwriting, policy and claims engines, with automated test cases per version, code changes only for genuinely new behaviour.

542
00:40:39.393 --> 00:40:40.481
Question 7.

543
00:40:40.761 --> 00:40:43.129
How would you handle aggregator quote traffic?

544
00:40:43.409 --> 00:40:44.774
Thirty-second answer:

545
00:40:45.054 --> 00:40:59.006
Stateless, horizontally scaled rating with in-memory tables, caching by risk signature, per-partner rate limits and quotas, fast external lookups with fallbacks, short quote retention, and monitoring conversion per partner.

546
00:41:00.356 --> 00:41:01.380
Question 8.

547
00:41:01.660 --> 00:41:03.388
How do claims workflows work?

548
00:41:03.668 --> 00:41:05.033
Thirty-second answer:

549
00:41:05.313 --> 00:41:22.081
A case moves through intake, coverage checks, triage, assignment, investigation, assessment, authority-based approval, payment and recovery, with human tasks, SLAs, reserves updated along the way, and straight-through processing for simple low-risk claims.

550
00:41:23.431 --> 00:41:24.498
Question 9.

551
00:41:24.778 --> 00:41:27.722
What is reinsurance and how does the system support it?

552
00:41:28.002 --> 00:41:29.367
Thirty-second answer:

553
00:41:29.647 --> 00:41:46.757
Insurers transfer part of their risk to reinsurers via treaties (quota share, surplus, excess of loss, catastrophe) or facultative deals; the system computes cessions per policy, recoveries per claim or event, and produces bordereaux and reinsurance accounting.

554
00:41:48.107 --> 00:41:48.875
Senior.

555
00:41:49.155 --> 00:41:50.200
Question 10.

556
00:41:50.480 --> 00:41:52.827
How would you price motor insurance with data?

557
00:41:53.107 --> 00:41:54.472
Thirty-second answer:

558
00:41:54.752 --> 00:42:14.571
Frequency (Poisson GLM) times severity (Gamma GLM) with log links giving multiplicative factors, credibility smoothing for sparse cells, ML to discover interactions then encoded in explainable tables, plus expense and profit loadings, within filed ranges, and monitor actual versus expected.

559
00:42:15.921 --> 00:42:17.051
Question 11.

560
00:42:17.331 --> 00:42:19.486
How do you detect insurance fraud rings?

561
00:42:19.766 --> 00:42:21.131
Thirty-second answer:

562
00:42:21.411 --> 00:42:38.307
Entity resolution across people, vehicles, providers, phones, bank accounts and addresses; build a graph; find communities and hubs; combine graph features with rules and supervised models; route high scores to the SIU; feed outcomes back as labels.

563
00:42:39.657 --> 00:42:40.745
Question 12.

564
00:42:41.025 --> 00:42:43.713
How would AI-assisted motor claims work safely?

565
00:42:43.993 --> 00:42:45.359
Thirty-second answer:

566
00:42:45.639 --> 00:43:03.772
Guided photo capture, metadata checks, part and damage detection models producing a repair estimate from parts catalogues and labour norms, confidence thresholds for auto-approval of small claims, surveys for the rest, and image-reuse and editing detection, with human override and monitoring.

567
00:43:05.122 --> 00:43:06.402
Question 13.

568
00:43:06.682 --> 00:43:10.693
How do you compute IBNR reserves, and what does the data platform provide?

569
00:43:10.973 --> 00:43:12.338
Thirty-second answer:

570
00:43:12.618 --> 00:43:30.495
Actuaries use development triangles (accident versus development period) and methods like chain ladder or Bornhuetter, Ferguson to project ultimate claims; IBNR is ultimate minus reported; the data platform must produce clean, reconciled triangles per product and period.

571
00:43:31.845 --> 00:43:33.040
Question 14.

572
00:43:33.320 --> 00:43:35.752
How do you audit hospital bills automatically?

573
00:43:36.032 --> 00:43:37.397
Thirty-second answer:

574
00:43:37.677 --> 00:43:54.893
Map items to standard codes, compare against package tariffs and cost benchmarks by diagnosis and city, apply policy rules (room-rent limits, co-pays, non-payables lists), flag duplicates and length-of-stay outliers, and route exceptions to medical officers.

575
00:43:56.243 --> 00:43:57.139
Architect.

576
00:43:57.419 --> 00:43:58.614
Question 15.

577
00:43:58.894 --> 00:44:01.646
Design a digital insurer's platform end to end.

578
00:44:01.926 --> 00:44:03.291
Thirty-second answer:

579
00:44:03.571 --> 00:44:23.838
Channel APIs for app, agents and aggregators; product configurator driving rating, underwriting, policy and claims engines; bitemporal policy administration; billing with mandates; claims workflows with STP, fraud scoring and SIU; reinsurance; Kafka to a lakehouse with actuarial marts,

580
00:44:24.118 --> 00:44:30.646
finance and regulatory reporting; ML for pricing, underwriting, triage, fraud and images.

581
00:44:31.996 --> 00:44:33.191
Question 16.

582
00:44:33.471 --> 00:44:35.540
Buy an insurance core suite or build?

583
00:44:35.820 --> 00:44:37.185
Thirty-second answer:

584
00:44:37.465 --> 00:44:47.471
Buy the heavily regulated, mature core when a suite fits; build the differentiating edges; insist on APIs, events and data access to avoid lock-in.

585
00:44:48.821 --> 00:44:50.165
Question 17.

586
00:44:50.445 --> 00:44:54.605
How would you migrate millions of in-force policies from a legacy system?

587
00:44:54.885 --> 00:44:56.250
Thirty-second answer:

588
00:44:56.530 --> 00:45:12.743
Map product versions and rules carefully, migrate by product or cohort with parallel runs comparing premiums, renewals and claims calculations, reconcile financials, keep legacy read-only for history, and plan cut-over around renewal cycles.

589
00:45:14.093 --> 00:45:15.267
Question 18.

590
00:45:15.547 --> 00:45:18.277
How do you prepare systems for a catastrophe event?

591
00:45:18.557 --> 00:45:19.923
Thirty-second answer:

592
00:45:20.203 --> 00:45:36.224
Catastrophe mode with simplified self-survey flows, surge assignment of surveyors, event tagging of claims, per-event reserves and reinsurance notifications, capacity for claim intake and document uploads, and dashboards for regulators and management.

593
00:45:37.574 --> 00:45:38.811
Question 19.

594
00:45:39.091 --> 00:45:42.441
How do regulations shape insurance systems in India?

595
00:45:42.721 --> 00:45:44.086
Thirty-second answer:

596
00:45:44.366 --> 00:46:06.979
Product approvals and filed rates constrain pricing; policyholder protection sets free-look, claim and grievance timelines; KYC for all policies; distribution and commission rules; data protection; solvency and reporting (and accounting standards) requirements, so rules and timelines must be configurable and auditable (VERIFY current texts).

597
00:46:08.329 --> 00:46:09.439
Question 20.

598
00:46:09.719 --> 00:46:14.220
What makes insurance data platforms harder than typical analytics platforms?

599
00:46:14.500 --> 00:46:15.865
Thirty-second answer:

600
00:46:16.145 --> 00:46:32.401
Long-lived contracts with bitemporal history, earned versus written measures, development over years, reconciliation with finance, actuarial triangles, regulatory formats, and model governance, errors directly change prices, reserves and solvency.

601
00:46:33.751 --> 00:46:34.797
Part 13.

602
00:46:35.077 --> 00:46:35.845
Summary.

603
00:46:37.095 --> 00:46:42.940
An insurer prices risk, administers contracts for years, pays claims and holds reserves.

604
00:46:43.220 --> 00:46:46.484
Each is a distinct system with its own correctness needs.

605
00:46:46.764 --> 00:46:53.079
Configuration-driven products and a bitemporal policy model make changes safe over long lifetimes.

606
00:46:53.359 --> 00:47:01.444
Claims are workflows: straight-through for simple cases, human expertise for the rest, fraud scoring and network analysis throughout.

607
00:47:01.724 --> 00:47:02.705
Algorithms:

608
00:47:02.985 --> 00:47:12.223
GLM pricing, rules engines, underwriting scores, telematics, computer vision for damage, fraud graphs, bill audits, chain-ladder reserving.

609
00:47:13.573 --> 00:47:17.925
The data platform serves pricing, reserving, finance and regulators.

610
00:47:18.205 --> 00:47:20.722
Data quality errors cost money directly.

611
